<!-- ============================================================
     Chapter: Reading Component Datasheets  (Day 5x)
     50-minute Wednesday x-hour, no pre-class reading.
     The part budgets below sum to 55 minutes, which is five more than the
     hour has.  Which five come out is a teaching call, not ours: CLAUDE.md
     fixes Day Nx at 50 minutes, and the week-5 budgets have been wrong three
     times already, so take the length from that line.
     ============================================================ -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-io-datasheets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Reading Component Datasheets</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to be able to pick up the datasheet of a
            component we have never used before and find in it everything we need in
            order to wire that component to the STM32C031C6 and write code that reads
            it or drives it.  Every useful embedded system connects the chip to the
            physical world through input and output components, and the datasheet is
            the only place that says what a given component needs and what it gives
            back.
            Microcontrollers have datasheets too, and we have used one already: the
            USART registers we programmed on Day 5 came out of the reference manual
            (<url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url>) and the
            <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">STM32C031C6 datasheet</url>.
            In this chapter we turn that same habit on the sensors and actuators
            around the chip.
        </p>
        <!-- check-rules: allow B-9  (the "three parts read over a digital
             protocol" are the DS3231, LIS3DH and Si7021, named in the answer key;
             the next list is <objectives>, which the count does not refer to) -->
        <p>
            This chapter covers one class day, a Wednesday x-hour, and it has no
            pre-class reading.  In class each table takes one real component and its
            datasheet, works through a worksheet on it, and presents what it found to
            the rest of us.  Between the five components we will see an analog
            voltage output, a part whose output is a signed quantity, and three parts
            that are read over a digital protocol.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Name the sections most datasheets have, and say which question each one answers.</li>
                <li>Find a part's supply voltage range and its absolute maximum ratings, and say both whether 3.3 V is safe and whether 5 V would destroy it.</li>
                <li>Say what interface a component uses, and list every pin needed for a minimal hookup.</li>
                <li>For a part with an analog output, write the transfer function that converts its output voltage into a physical quantity; for an I2C part, list the addresses it can take.</li>
                <li>Decide from a part's current or power numbers whether it can be powered from a GPIO pin.</li>
                <li>Name one thing that has to be configured or calculated in code before a component can be read.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-datasheets-inclass">
        <title>In-Class: Datasheet Scavenger Hunt</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll learn to read a component's datasheet.  The worksheet below
                asks of one real component the questions we would have to answer before
                wiring it up and programming it, and each table answers them for a
                different component, so between us we will have read five datasheets
                rather than one.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- ── Opening (≈5 min) ──────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-datasheets-intro">
            <title>Embedded Systems Need Peripherals</title>
            <p>
                An embedded system is a microcontroller together with the components it
                reads and drives (<xref ref="fig-mcu-io-block"/>).  The STM32C031C6
                measures nothing and moves nothing on its own.  It reads
                <em>input</em> components, which turn something in the physical world
                into a signal the chip can read, and it drives <em>output</em>
                components, which change something in the physical world.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-mcu-io-block">
                <caption>Top-level view of an embedded system.  Sensors,
                switches, and data streams feed into the microcontroller through
                interfaces; the microcontroller sends commands out to displays,
                actuators, and data streams through interfaces on the other side.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day05X-InputOutputDatasheets/slide04_img1.png"
                       width="75%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Getting a peripheral to work requires answers to a set of questions,
                and for most of them the component's <term>datasheet</term> is the only
                place to look.  What supply voltage does it need, and what are the
                minimum and maximum operating conditions it can tolerate?  What signal
                does it produce or consume, and what does that signal actually
                represent?  Does the chip do its own analog-to-digital conversion and
                give you numbers over a digital protocol such as I2C (Inter-Integrated
                Circuit) or SPI, or does it output a raw voltage that the
                analog-to-digital converter (ADC) built into the STM32C031C6 must
                interpret?  Is there a built-in analog front end, or do
                you need external signal conditioning?  How should you wire it up, and
                what does your code need to configure before it will respond?  These are
                a few of the essential questions, but not the only ones.
            </p>
            <p>
                Datasheets can be intimidating: they are written for engineers who
                already know the jargon, and they pack enormous amounts of information
                into dense tables.  They also vary between component types and between
                manufacturers.  Reading one takes patience.  What makes it manageable is
                that most datasheets are organized along the same lines, so once we know
                which section answers which question, we can find our way around a
                datasheet we have never opened before.
            </p>

            <insight xml:id="insight-datasheet-sections">
                <title>Where to Look in a Datasheet</title>
                <p>
                    The first page typically shows at least the high-level picture:
                    <em>Features</em> (a bullet list of what the part does)
                    and a <em>Description</em> paragraph (always read this:
                    it names the interface and application).  The body then
                    contains, roughly in this order:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Pinout / Pin descriptions</em>: which pin is which,
                    and what each one does.
                    <em>Absolute Maximum Ratings</em>: never exceed these;
                    doing so destroys the part.
                    <em>Recommended Operating Conditions</em>: the range
                    the part is designed to work in.
                    <em>Electrical Characteristics</em>: voltages, currents,
                    timing, in a table with Min / Typ / Max columns.
                    <em>Application Information</em> (sometimes called
                    "Theory of Operation" or "Detailed Description"),
                    the most important section for a designer: example
                    schematics, register maps, and usage notes.
                    <em>Packaging Information</em>: physical dimensions
                    of the available package options (DIP, SOT-23, QFN,
                    etc.).  Rarely relevant for breadboard work, but
                    critical if you are designing a PCB.
                </p>
                <p>
                    One notation to watch for: a pin name with a bar over it
                    (or a star next to it in a table) is <em>active low</em>:
                    you pull it to ground to activate it, rather than
                    driving it high.
                </p>
            </insight>


            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-peripherals" ref="fig-mcu-io-block">
                <ul>
                    <li>An embedded system is a microcontroller together with the components it reads and drives. The STM32C031C6 measures nothing and moves nothing on its own.</li>
                    <li>It reads <em>input</em> components, which turn something in the physical world into a signal the chip can read.</li>
                    <li>It drives <em>output</em> components, which change something in the physical world.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>Inputs feed the microcontroller through interfaces; the microcontroller sends commands out through interfaces on the other side.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-questions">
                <ul>
                    <li>What supply voltage does it need, and what minimum and maximum conditions can it tolerate?</li>
                    <li>What signal does it produce or consume, and what does that signal represent?</li>
                    <li>Does it do its own conversion and give you numbers over a digital protocol such as I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit) or SPI, or does it output a raw voltage that the STM32C031C6's analog-to-digital converter (ADC) must interpret?</li>
                    <li>How do you wire it up, and what must your code configure before it responds?</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-skeleton">
                <p>Datasheets vary between component types and manufacturers, and reading one takes patience.  What makes it manageable is that most are organized along the same lines.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>First page, typically: Features (what it does) and Description (always read this: names the interface and application).</li>
                    <li>Pinout / Pin descriptions: which pin is which.</li>
                    <li>Absolute Maximum Ratings: never exceed these; doing so destroys the part.</li>
                    <li>Recommended Operating Conditions: the range it's designed for.</li>
                    <li>Electrical Characteristics: voltages, currents, timing, with Min / Typ / Max columns.</li>
                    <li>Application Information, the most important section for a designer: example schematics, register maps, usage notes.</li>
                    <li>Packaging: physical dimensions (rarely needed for breadboards).</li>
                    <li>Watch for active-low: a bar over a pin name (or a star in a table) means you pull it to ground to activate it.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Scavenger Hunt Activity (≈45 min) ─────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-datasheets-scavenger">
            <title>Datasheet Scavenger Hunt</title>
            <p>
                We'll give each table one component to study.  Open its datasheet from
                Canvas, display it where your whole table can see it, and work through
                the Datasheet Worksheet below.  At the end of class each table
                presents its answers to the rest of us.
            </p>
            <!-- The five are Petra's own list (2026-08-14), and the parts the
                 course studies later are deliberately NOT among them: the
                 LSM303AGR is Day 13's chapter and the photocell is Day 16's, so
                 sending a table to look either one up here would spend the
                 unfamiliarity this activity is built on. Do not "fix" this list
                 by adding a part because the course uses it elsewhere, which is
                 backwards, and it is how the invented list got here. Datasheets
                 for all five are in assets/datasheets/. -->
            <p>
                The components, one per table:
                the <url href="external/datasheets/tmp235.pdf">TMP235</url> (analog temperature sensor),
                the <url href="external/datasheets/drv5053.pdf">DRV5053</url> (analog Hall-effect magnetic sensor),
                the <url href="external/datasheets/ds3231.pdf">DS3231</url> (I2C real-time clock),
                the <url href="external/datasheets/lis3dh.pdf">LIS3DH</url> (I2C or SPI three-axis accelerometer),
                and the <url href="external/datasheets/si7021.pdf">Si7021</url> (I2C humidity and temperature sensor).
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-datasheet-passport">
                <title>The Datasheet Worksheet</title>

                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Work through Parts A–D, then build your group slide
                        in Part E.  Some questions have two versions
                        depending on whether your component is analog or
                        digital.  Answer the one that applies to your component.
                        If you finish early, sketch on your table's whiteboard
                        how you would connect your component to the
                        Nucleo-C031C6.
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-a">
                    <title>Part A: What Is It?</title>
                    <p><em>Answers to these questions are typically on the first page.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Read the <em>Description</em> (not the
                                Features list).  In one sentence, what does
                                this component do?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the <em>Recommended Operating
                                Conditions</em>.  What supply voltage range
                                does the part accept?  (The supply pin is
                                labeled VDD or VCC, depending on the
                                manufacturer.)  Is 3.3 V within that range?
                                (For passive components with no supply pin,
                                find the maximum rated operating voltage
                                and power dissipation instead.)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the <em>Absolute Maximum Ratings</em>
                                table.  What is the maximum voltage the
                                part can survive?  Are there any other
                                limits listed that a careless user might
                                accidentally exceed?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <!-- Part B's checklist offers seven interfaces; Part C below
                     branches for four of them (passive, analog, I2C, SPI).  A table
                     that honestly checks UART, PWM or "Other" has nowhere to go on
                     Part C.  It does not bite this term, because none of the five
                     assigned parts is UART- or PWM-driven, which is why it has
                     survived, but the two lists disagree about how many interface
                     types exist.  Which way to close that is a worksheet-design
                     question for Petra (trim the checklist to four, or give Part C an
                     escape-hatch branch), so do not "fix" it by guessing: it changes
                     what students are asked.  Found by learner-in-the-room,
                     2026-09-15; see reviews/io-datasheets-learner-gate.md. -->
                <task xml:id="task-passport-b">
                    <title>Part B: How Does It Communicate?</title>
                    <p><em>Answers to these questions are typically in the Pinout and Application sections.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                What interface does this component use?
                                Check one, or more if it supports several:
                            </p>
                            <p><ul>
                                <li><em>Passive</em>, a variable resistance.</li>
                                <li><em>Analog voltage output.</em></li>
                                <li><em>I2C</em>, Inter-Integrated Circuit.</li>
                                <li><em>SPI</em>, Serial Peripheral Interface.</li>
                                <li><em>UART</em>, or plain serial.</li>
                                <li><em>PWM</em>, pulse width modulation, as an input.</li>
                                <li><em>Something else.</em></li>
                            </ul></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                List every pin needed for a minimal hookup.
                                For each pin, write its name and what it
                                connects to (VDD, GND, a specific pin of the
                                STM32C031C6, and so on).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-c">
                    <title>Part C: What Does It Output or Require?</title>
                    <p><em>Answers to these questions are typically in the Electrical Characteristics table.</em></p>
                    <p>
                        <em>If your component is a passive sensor
                        (variable resistance):</em>
                        What is the resistance at the minimum and
                        maximum of its measurement range?  Is the
                        relationship between resistance and the
                        measured quantity linear, or does the
                        datasheet give a curve or formula?
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <em>If your component has an analog voltage
                        output:</em>
                        What is the output voltage at the minimum
                        and maximum of its measurement range?
                        Is the relationship linear?  Write the
                        transfer function, the equation that
                        converts output voltage to a physical value
                        (temperature, light level, etc.).
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <em>If your component uses I2C:</em>
                        What address(es) can the part use?
                        Many parts have a fixed base address with
                        one or two address pins (A0, A1) that let
                        you set the last bits, so multiple copies
                        of the same chip can share a bus.  List
                        the possible addresses.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <em>If your component uses SPI:</em>
                        What SPI mode does it require (clock
                        polarity CPOL and clock phase CPHA)?
                        What is the maximum clock frequency it
                        supports?
                    </p>
                </task>

                <!-- Part C is split across two tasks so that each one fits on
                     its projected slide with its writing room intact. As a
                     single task it ran 68px past the bottom of the frame and
                     the current question was clipped. -->
                <task xml:id="task-passport-c2">
                    <title>Part C, continued: Current and Power</title>
                    <p><em>Answers to these questions are also typically in the Electrical Characteristics table.</em></p>
                    <p>
                        What is the maximum current the part draws
                        from its supply?  Does that rule out
                        powering it from a GPIO pin (max 25 mA)?
                        (For passive components with no supply pin,
                        find the maximum power dissipation rating
                        instead.)
                    </p>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-d">
                    <title>Part D: Making It Work</title>
                    <p><em>Answers to these questions are typically in the Application Information section.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the example schematic or application
                                circuit.  Sketch the minimal hookup on your
                                table's whiteboard: just power, ground and the
                                signal lines, each labeled with its pin name.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Name one thing you would need to configure
                                or calculate in code before you could
                                read data from this component.  (Examples:
                                set the I2C address, choose a gain setting,
                                apply a conversion formula to the raw
                                ADC reading.)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                What was the most confusing or surprising
                                thing you found in the datasheet?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-slide">
                    <title>Part E: Group Slide</title>
                    <p>
                        Add one slide for your component to the shared class
                        deck (link on Canvas).  Include your answers to all
                        of Parts A–D, everything except the board sketch.
                        Keep it concise: use short phrases, not paragraphs.
                        The goal is for other groups to be able to use the
                        information you provide as a reference.
                    </p>
                </task>

            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-datasheet-worked">
                <p>
                    What Part E asks for is Parts A–D, everything except the
                    board sketch.  The sketch stays on the table's whiteboard and
                    out of the slide because drawing one on a slide, live, costs
                    more of the hour than it returns.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Below is one table's slide from the year this ran, on the
                    TMP23x analog temperature sensor, reproduced as they wrote
                    it.  Read it as the calibration for <q>concise</q> (short
                    phrases, no paragraphs) rather than for coverage: it answers
                    an earlier and shorter brief, so a slide meeting the current
                    Part E carries more than this, including the supply range,
                    the absolute maximums and the current draw.
                </p>
                <p><ul>
                    <li><em>Name, model, manufacturer.</em>  TMP23x temperature
                        sensor, Texas Instruments.</li>
                    <li><em>Description.</em>  Linear analog output; voltage
                        proportional to temperature over −40 °C to 150 °C, at
                        10 mV per °C.</li>
                    <li><em>How to connect it.</em>  Output to an ADC input on
                        the microcontroller; may want a series resistor and
                        capacitor to filter noise, depending on what the ADC
                        input needs.</li>
                    <li><em>How to communicate with it.</em>  There is no
                        protocol: the output <em>is</em> a voltage, so reading
                        it means converting it.</li>
                    <li><em>Anything else crucial.</em>  Which pins can do
                        analog: A0–A5, D10–D13, D4.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p>
                    Two things it does right even so, and both are worth naming
                    at the share-out.  It gives the transfer function as a
                    number a program can use, 10 mV per °C, rather than
                    <q>outputs a voltage</q>.  And its last line is about
                    <em>our</em> board, not the component: the group checked
                    which of the Nucleo-C031C6's pins could receive the signal.  That is Part D's
                    question in practice.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Parts A–D are component-specific, and their answers come out
                    of each table's own datasheet rather than from here.  The
                    worksheet already names the section each answer is in, which
                    is the part of the habit that carries over: <em>Description</em> and
                    <em>Recommended Operating Conditions</em> on the first pages,
                    <em>Absolute Maximum Ratings</em>, the pinout, <em>Electrical
                    Characteristics</em>, and <em>Application Information</em>
                    about halfway through.
                </p>
            </instructor>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-datasheet-key">
                <p>
                    Below are the answers to Parts A–D for the five components
                    whose datasheets are in the repository
                    (<c>assets/datasheets/</c>).  Every number
                    below is cited to the table it came from, because that is the
                    habit Parts A–D are meant to build, and the tables move between
                    revisions.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part A2 and A3 at a glance.</term>  Every one of the
                    five accepts 3.3 V, which is the answer to A2 in each case,
                    but the interesting column is the last one, because two of
                    these parts are destroyed by 5 V and three are not.
                </p>
                <tabular top="major" left="minor" bottom="minor">
                    <row bottom="major">
                        <cell>Part</cell><cell>Supply range (A2)</cell>
                        <cell>Absolute max supply (A3)</cell><cell>Survives 5 V?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row><cell>TMP235</cell><cell>2.3–5.5 V (§6.3)</cell>
                        <cell>+6 V (§6.1)</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>DS3231</cell><cell>2.3–5.5 V (Recommended Operating Conditions)</cell>
                        <cell>−0.3 to +6.0 V on any pin</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>DRV5053-Q1</cell><cell>2.7–38 V (§6.3)</cell>
                        <cell>−22 to +40 V (§6.1)</cell><cell>yes, easily</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Si7021-A20</cell><cell>1.9–3.6 V (Table 1)</cell>
                        <cell>+4.2 V (Table 7)</cell><cell><em>no</em></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="major"><cell>LIS3DH</cell><cell>1.71–3.6 V (Table 6)</cell>
                        <cell>+4.8 V (Table 9)</cell><cell><em>no</em></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <p>
                    That column is worth having the tables read out at the
                    share-out.  A student
                    who has only ever seen 5 V parts will reach for the 5 V rail
                    out of habit, and on two of these five that is a destroyed
                    component rather than a wrong reading.  It is also the reason
                    the Absolute Maximum table is a separate question from the
                    Recommended Operating Conditions table: the first says what
                    kills it, the second says where it works.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part C2, supply current.</term>  None of the five comes
                    close to the 25 mA a GPIO pin can source, so the answer to
                    <q>does that rule out powering it from a pin</q> is no in
                    every case: TMP235 9 µA typical (§6.5), LIS3DH 11 µA in
                    normal mode at 50 Hz (Table 6), Si7021 150 µA while
                    converting but <em>4 mA peak</em> during power-up and I2C
                    activity (Table 2), DS3231 200 µA active at 3.63 V, and
                    DRV5053 2.7 mA typical (§6.5), the largest of the five, and
                    still a tenth of the budget.  The honest follow-up is that
                    <q>it fits</q> is not the same as <q>it is a good idea</q>:
                    the Si7021's peak is fifteen times its running current, and
                    a pin that also has to stay at a clean logic level is not the
                    place to put that.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part C, worked for the analog branch: the TMP235.</term>
                    The transfer function is in §6.5: the sensor gain is
                    10 mV/°C and the output at 0 °C is 500 mV, both for the
                    TMP235 specifically.  The TMP236 in the same document is
                    19.5 mV/°C and 400 mV, which is the first place a table
                    reader goes wrong.  So
                </p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
V_OUT(mV)  =  500  +  10 x T(degC)          T(degC)  =  (V_OUT(mV) - 500) / 10
]]></code></program>
                <p>
                    At the ends of the −40 °C to +150 °C range that is 100 mV and
                    2.000 V, so the whole output swing falls inside our 3.3 V ADC
                    range with room to spare and needs no divider.  It is linear:
                    §6.5 gives output nonlinearity as ±0.5 °C, which is a
                    deviation from the straight line rather than a curve to look
                    up.  One count of our 12-bit ADC is 3.3 V / 4096 = 0.806 mV,
                    which is 0.08 °C: the converter is roughly thirty times
                    finer than the sensor's own ±2.5 °C accuracy, and that
                    comparison is the one worth drawing.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part C, worked for the I2C branch: the Si7021.</term>
                    §5 gives the 7-bit base address as <c>0x40</c>, and Table 10
                    shows all seven bits fixed: there are no address pins, so
                    two of these cannot share a bus.  Contrast the LIS3DH, whose
                    address is <c>0011000b</c> with the SDO/SA0 pin selecting the
                    low bit: 0x18 with SA0 grounded, 0x19 with it tied to the
                    supply (§6.1.1), which is exactly the <q>list the possible
                    addresses</q> the worksheet asks for.  The DS3231's is
                    <c>1101000b</c>, 0x68, fixed.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part D, the code question.</term>  For an analog part
                    it is the conversion: the ADC gives a count, and something
                    has to turn it into millivolts and then into °C or mT.  For a
                    digital part it is a command sequence: the Si7021 needs
                    <q>measure temperature</q> (0xE3 or 0xF3, Table 11) sent
                    before anything can be read back, and the 16-bit code that
                    comes back is not a temperature until it has been through the
                    equation in §5.1.2.  Both are the same lesson in two
                    dialects: the number on the wire is never the physical
                    quantity.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>The DRV5053.</term>
                    Its output is 0.2 V to 1.8 V with about 1 V at zero field
                    (§6.7), so the quantity it measures is <em>signed</em> (north
                    and south push the output opposite ways from a 1 V rest
                    point), while the ADC only ever returns a non-negative count.
                    A student who reports <q>output 0.2 to 1.8 V</q> and stops has
                    answered C correctly and missed what makes it hard.  Its
                    sensitivity is also not one number: the part comes in
                    variants from −11 to −90 mV/mT (and two positive ones), so
                    <q>the sensitivity</q> depends on which suffix is printed on
                    the package in front of them.
                </p>
            </instructor>


            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-assign">
                <ul>
                    <li>Open your component's datasheet from Canvas and display it where your whole table can see it.</li>
                    <li>At the end of class each table presents its answers to the rest of us.</li>
                    <li>The components, one per table: TMP235 (analog temperature), DRV5053 (analog Hall effect), DS3231 (I2C real-time clock), LIS3DH (I2C accelerometer), Si7021 (I2C humidity and temperature).</li>
                    <li>Some questions have an analog and a digital version.  Answer the one that applies to your component.  If you finish early, sketch the hookup to the Nucleo-C031C6 on your table's whiteboard.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Share-out (≈5 min) ────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-datasheets-shareout">
            <title>Share-Out</title>
            <p>
                In your share-out, cover at least these three things:
            </p>
            <p><ol>
                <li><p>Component name and what it does (one sentence).</p></li>
                <li><p>Interface type: passive, analog, I2C, SPI, or other.</p></li>
                <li><p>One thing that surprised you or that others should know.</p></li>
            </ol></p>
            <p>
                As you listen, note which of the five components give an analog output
                and which are read over a digital protocol.  That split decides how a
                component gets read: an analog part goes to one of our ADC inputs and we
                convert the count into a physical quantity ourselves, while a digital
                part hands back a number it has already converted, and our job is to ask
                it for that number over I2C or SPI.  We'll come back to several of these
                parts later in the term.
            </p>


            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-shareout">
                <p>In your share-out, cover at least these three things:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>The component's name and what it does, in one sentence.</li>
                    <li>Its interface: passive, analog, I2C, SPI, or something else.</li>
                    <li>One thing that surprised you, or that the rest of us should know.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>As you listen, note which of the five give an analog output and which are read over a digital protocol.</p>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

    </section>

</chapter>
